;;; P1.21 R4 / M1a S1a.4.1 — the lookahead's NAF world includes the candidate.
;;;
;;; The one-step lookahead asks "does adding `h` to the saturated KB
;;; produce a contradiction in a single rule firing?" by *positing* `h`
;;; into a positive premise and running the rule's other premises against
;;; the KB. Its contract is one-sided: it may miss a death (the branch
;;; then forks and dies normally), but it must never report a live
;;; hypothesis as dead — that loss is silent.
;;;
;;; `self-block` is the shape that used to break it. Its two premises are
;;; jointly unsatisfiable in any one world, so it can never fire in a real
;;; match — yet the probe posited `h` into `(cand ?x)` while evaluating
;;; `(absent (cand ?x))` against a KB *without* `h`. The NAF answered
;;; about a different world than the probe hypothesised, the guard
;;; "passed", `(false)` was derived, and every `cand` candidate was killed.
;;;
;;; Since S1.21.8 the guard is judged in the world `kb` **plus** `h`: it
;;; must find no match in `kb` *and* `h` must not create one. Here `h` is
;;; exactly `(cand B)`, so it creates one, the guard fails, and the
;;; candidate survives.
;;;
;;; What makes this a *whole-solve* fixture and not just a filter one is the
;;; kill cache. A killed candidate is written back as `(not h)`, so a wrong
;;; kill does not merely skip a branch — it poisons the root: `(not (cand B))`
;;; lands there, the branch that would have derived `(cand B)` dies against
;;; it, every branch dies, and the verdict flips.
;;;
;;; Measured with `ein solve -e -s`, 2026-08-18:
;;;
;;;   as shipped                          Solution, ?x = A, 2 enterings
;;;   guard judged against `kb` alone     Contradiction, k = 0, 0 enterings
;;;
;;; `cand` is rule-producible (`promote` concludes it), so the auto-closure
;;; would leave it guessable — though note `emit_closed` runs only on a fork
;;; under `--hyp-stats` and the JSON summary, never on the `solve` path
;;; itself, so what the search actually sees is every relation open.

(config :enable-pre-branch-lookahead true)

(relation ok      T)
(relation blessed T)
(relation cand    T)

(rule promote ()
  :match  (and (ok ?x) (blessed ?x))
  :assert (cand ?x)
  :why    "{?x} is ok and blessed, so it is a candidate"
  :priority 100)

(rule self-block ()
  :match  (and (cand ?x) (absent (cand ?x)))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "unsatisfiable in any one world"
  :priority 200)

(ok      A :source "(1)")
(ok      B :source "(2)")
(blessed A :source "(3)")

(query
  :goal (cand ?x))
